#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130300} :activity/id #uuid "bf654963-a5d1-5562-85bc-b7c2fbbe6d40" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049265 :topic/id #uuid "e80c9bca-b203-5866-835e-d0d29bbfdf22" :topic/math-academy-id 1995} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130300 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130449 :tutorial/content "Recall that when we rotate a curve by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, the area generated by this surface of revolution is given by\n\n$$\nS =  \\int 2\\pi y \\, \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow suppose we have a parametrically defined curve: \n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad\ny = y(t), \\qquad\nt \\in \\left[t_1,t_2\\right].\n$$\n\nIn the case of a parametric curve, the arc length element $\\textrm{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{d}s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following formula for the surface area generated by rotating a parametrically defined curve about the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y(t) \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "36a57433-1c78-58b8-860c-0ea6e3471761" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6113 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c5c27e9e-03cb-5a93-8a56-bd33588960fe" :step/math-academy-id 618749 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130301}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130301 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130466 :tutorial/content "Consider the curve defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = \\cos{t}, \\qquad\ny = \\sin{t}, \\qquad\nt \\in [0,\\pi].\n$$\n\nIf we rotate this curve by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we obtain a sphere of radius $1.$ \n\n![](images/d0/d0d323b764b70d3f2830d821636a9bc27b40e7f7e9f45df909c81dd279dc16e4.png)\n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of our surface of revolution can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-2\\pi (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-2\\pi (-1-1) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f849fe7f-670f-5a7f-8dc7-dbbd33a876f1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7707 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "031d5fb3-1834-595b-8a36-577430a0f9a7" :step/math-academy-id 618750 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130302}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130302 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129318 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129073 :question/id #uuid "066f76be-83f6-52fd-ab3f-00786d18f64e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9866"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b02082a8-b56e-5478-859d-51db7273f765" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050860}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435125} {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218652